      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "Although it is well recognised that patients with ischemic heart disease can have normal coronary arteries on coronary angiography, most such patients have angina pectoris, whilst a minority have had a previous myocardial infarction. There are few reports of patients with recurrent myocardial infarctions and angina, but with normal coronary arteries on coronary angiography. We describe six patients who had more than one myocardial infarction, confirmed by raised cardiac enzymes and changes on the electrocardiogram. They subsequently developed classical angina and subsequent coronary angiography demonstrated no atherosclerotic coronary artery disease. Coronary artery spasm and diminished coronary reserve may have been contributory factors in these patients."},
